  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 155 AM CDT Tue Oct 27 2026\n\nThe cold front that shifted across our area yesterday morning \nhas stalled across central Illinois and Indiana. Light winds \nand low dew point depressions near the front may support a brief\nperiod of early morning fog, primarily across the southern \ncounties. We will continue to monitor this overnight, but \nwidespread dense fog appears unlikely.\n\nDuring the day today, this front will lift back northward as a\nwarm front as a shortwave impulse over Kansas and Oklahoma\ntracks northeastward across the Mid and Upper Mississippi\nValley. This impulse will bring extensive mid- and upper-level\ncloud cover through the day, but a drier low-level airmass\nshould keep conditions rain-free. Despite the cloud cover, warm\nair advection will push temperatures inland into the upper 60s\nto low 70s this afternoon. Closer to the lake, southeasterly\nwinds ahead of the approaching front will favor an afternoon\nlake breeze into northeastern Illinois, slowing the warm \nfront's progress and keeping temperatures several degrees \ncooler than inland areas.\n\nAfter the warm front shifts north of the area tonight, \nWednesday is shaping up to be unseasonably mild area-wide. High\ntemperatures are forecast in the low to mid-70s Wednesday \nafternoon.\n\nThe next notable shortwave impulse is expected to track well \nnorth across southern Canada Wednesday night. As an associated \nsurface low moves eastward across Ontario, a southwestward-\ntrailing cold front will push southward into our area by \nThursday. Although the strongest large-scale forcing will pass \nwell to the north, pooling low-level moisture (dew points in \nthe mid to upper 50s) along the frontal boundary could support \nisolated to widely scattered showers, particularly late \nWednesday night into Thursday. Consequently, we maintain low- \nend (2030%) chances of precipitation during this period.\n\nTemperatures will turn slightly cooler for the end of the week\nbehind this front. However, with no strong cold airmass behind\nThursday's front, temperatures should remain above average\nthrough the weekend. Periodic rain chances (and possibly a few\nthunderstorms) will increase late in the weekend and into next\nweek. Ensemble guidance suggests a series of impulses will \ntrack from Mexico east-northeastward into the Lower Great Lakes \nregion during the weekend, followed by a potentially more \nsubstantial system tracking slowly across the central U.S. next \nweek. As a result, there will be daily chances for rain from \nSaturday into next week. Dry periods are still expected, so it \nwill not be raining continuously throughout the weekend.\n\nKJB",
